      "role": "user",
      "content": "danton and sieyes seem a bit too loud/public for what i feel. what i remember is more quiet, subtle power. like being the person sitting just behind the main figure, the one who whispered the right thing at the right time to make a treaty work or prevent a scandal. it was about navigating the fine lines of etiquette and using information as a tool. \n\ncan you dig deeper into the specific roles of aides-de-camp or secret secretaries to the ministry during that time? specifically someone who had to handle high-stakes correspondence and maintain a very composed, almost detached exterior while managing intense political tension. i feel like if i can find a specific person whose daily pressures mirror mine, i'll know if i'm actually remembering something.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
